    {
      "post_id": 17701,
      "author_username": "meizzwang",
      "author_user_id": 11,
      "timestamp_iso": "2015-05-11T22:23:38+00:00",
      "timestamp_epoch_ms": 1431383018000,
      "timestamp_raw": "May 11, 2015 17:23:38 GMT -5",
      "body_html": "Here's some pictures of various clones that I haven't yet given a letter or number designation to because there's just too many.  These plants are genetically diverse, and they didn't look all that great for a few years because they suffered from poor water quality, which made them all green.<br/><br/><br/>Some of them this year are still greenish looking, but don't be fooled, all of these have turned solid bright red for many years straight in the past.  This year's traps turned out to be really beautiful, and there's a few stunners in the crowd.  And just to preempt some inquiries, I do have divisions of all of these, but they're mixed together.  I propagate the best ones  (this is what happens when you have too many plants and high standards), so only nice clones go out.  Photos taken 5/10/15:<br/><br/><br/>Check out the color on this one!<br/><img src=\"https://c1.staticflickr.com/9/8870/16890223084_13b6a2dc84_z.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/>Look at the shape of the lid on this one:<br/><img src=\"https://c1.staticflickr.com/9/8717/16890204334_4671726c25_z.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"https://c2.staticflickr.com/8/7702/17325160240_1a804027c6_z.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"https://c2.staticflickr.com/8/7749/17512532221_0ab14ebb1e_z.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"https://c2.staticflickr.com/8/7701/17325204998_8f917a0c7d_z.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><br/>Lots of these traps fell over in the move (In the previous photo, I was propping a few of the traps up, you can't tell):<br/><img src=\"https://c2.staticflickr.com/8/7675/17324883988_6b73ba7a06_z.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><br/><br/>",
      "body_text": "Here's some pictures of various clones that I haven't yet given a letter or number designation to because there's just too many.  These plants are genetically diverse, and they didn't look all that great for a few years because they suffered from poor water quality, which made them all green.\nSome of them this year are still greenish looking, but don't be fooled, all of these have turned solid bright red for many years straight in the past.  This year's traps turned out to be really beautiful, and there's a few stunners in the crowd.  And just to preempt some inquiries, I do have divisions of all of these, but they're mixed together.  I propagate the best ones  (this is what happens when you have too many plants and high standards), so only nice clones go out.  Photos taken 5/10/15:\nCheck out the color on this one!\nLook at the shape of the lid on this one:\nLots of these traps fell over in the move (In the previous photo, I was propping a few of the traps up, you can't tell):",
